WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:09.440
LAURA KIESEL: I'm Laura Kiesel.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.570
And I have chronic pain, and I work as a freelance writer and editor.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.430
The onset of chronic pain was really gradual for me.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:22.970
It didn't happen suddenly with an accident or anything in particular.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.600
I was born with a really severe case of clubfoot.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.555
I had to have, as a child, several surgeries

00:00:29.555 --> 00:00:32.930
and wear a cast till I was three.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:38.840
And that left me with some structural discrepancies and issues in my body.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:44.900
Like my leg is shorter on my left side than my right, and the muscles in it

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:45.980
are underdeveloped.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.580
And the foot has no arch and is sort of pronated.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:54.590
So over time as I got older, I had more and more stress

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:57.890
on my hips and my low spine.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:02.480
So I always had some pain as a child and as a teenager,

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.910
but it hasn't been until the past several years

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:09.060
that it's become just daily.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.560
It was usually more sporadic or intermittent growing up.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.580
I also have, unrelated to that, endometriosis,

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.530
which is a disease, a reproductive disease, related to menstruation.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:26.720
So starting in high school I started getting very painful periods.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.520
And it wasn't until I was 24 that I was able to finally get surgery

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.900
to have a lot of it removed and have it diagnosed.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.990
So I had pain during those days, and I had some other pain.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:41.840
But it wasn't daily until the past few years,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.990
where I've started to have a lot of degenerative changes in my hips

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:45.830
and spine.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:50.300
They think partially from that discrepancy from my legs,

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:55.040
and just the wear and tear as you grow older of walking,

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.110
with those kind of structural abnormalities

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.970
kind of wearing on my body.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:02.360
So when pain becomes a daily reality, it can

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:07.710
be a very hollowing, almost dehumanizing experience after a while,

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.410
because it's just--

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:10.970
you wake up and you're still in pain.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.780
And you go to bed and you're still in pain.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.310
And you don't sleep well because you're in pain.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.640
And so it can be very distracting.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.390
I think it's very hard until you've had that be your daily reality

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:29.810
to realize how much it can affect your concentration, affect your mobility,

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:34.980
affect your ability to do things that you took for granted.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.050
So many things that used to come very easily to me now take a lot of effort

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.930
and even sometimes just bandwidth.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:45.440
Like the mental or emotional bandwidth to deal with something

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.270
when I have a really bad pain day is much lower on some days than others.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:55.280
Some days I just can't handle a workload at all and I just need to be in bed

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:56.211
or just take it easy.

00:02:56.211 --> 00:02:57.710
And other days I'll have a good day.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:01.790
But even if I'm very productive, usually it's like then I run on a deficit

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.250
the following days for having a high-energy day.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.250
It's never-- nothing is ever free anymore.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:12.620
It's like you're running constantly on a certain amount of borrowed time

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:19.040
and bandwidth and physical energy or tolerance to pain.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.930
I've only been offered not even really what's

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:29.360
considered pain medication, primarily, but things like anticonvulsants,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:36.210
like Gabapentin and Lyrica, which are primarily for epilepsy,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:40.230
but now are being used for people with fibromyalgia and nerve pain.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:41.780
So I've tried those.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.630
I didn't have-- they didn't really help much.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.560
They took the edge off a little but not much.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.940
And then the side effects kind of outweighed any benefits.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:53.630
So I stopped taking them after a few months.

00:03:53.630 --> 00:04:03.070
Other than NSAIDs, or like ibuprofen and Tylenol,

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:05.960
like I don't really have much else that's been offered to me.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.297
The only time opioids have really been offered to me--

00:04:08.297 --> 00:04:10.130
I know now they're cutting back, so it might

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:14.270
be the timing with which my chronic pain has really blown up--

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:18.230
has been really acute situations, where they've only given me a few days worth.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:25.220
Like when my disc first herniated, I got a few days worth of codeine.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.380
The only thing I take semi-regularly is I

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:31.100
take Flexeril, which is a muscle relaxant, a few nights a week

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:34.940
when I really need to try to sleep through the pain.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.270
Most people I know in my chronic pain group, and friends

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.600
who have had similar issues, are on opioids or at least

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.880
were for a time, an extended period of time.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.120
But because I have a family history of addiction, a really prevalent family

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:54.410
history of addiction to opioid drugs, I did not want to take that.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.720
So I've never really inquired.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:02.390
And even when I have for very acute situations, like when my disc first

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:07.130
got injured, I made sure to ask for the lowest type and the lowest dose.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:11.160
Because I just don't want to even risk going down any kind of pathway

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.620
to addiction, similar to my family members.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:16.860
My mother was a heroin addict.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.030
She did not start with opioids.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:25.620
She started straight from experimenting with different drugs like cocaine,

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.030
and then started with heroin and she became a heroin addict.,

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.820
So my mother was a heroin addict for many years.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:38.940
And then she got on a methadone program, and she wasn't on heroin anymore.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.640
But when she became a cancer patient--

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.680
and she had terminal cancer for many years.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.360
It was terminal but it was five years before she died--

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.340
she was given an opioid prescription.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.330
I don't think they did any evaluation of her history.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:57.180
Or maybe they also figured just because she is terminal,

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:58.800
to overlook her history.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.260
But my brother was in the household with her.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:08.610
She started giving him her pills, so he became addicted to Dilaudid.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:13.200
And when she died, he actually suffered very badly from withdrawal

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.860
because he suddenly didn't have access to her prescriptions anymore.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:19.620
And he took to the streets and he got arrested.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:24.870
And he finally had to check himself in for a couple of days.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:29.330
And then he got on Suboxone and got off opioids.

